Step-by-Step Guide to Install VirtualBox and Create a Kali Linux VM with NAT

You can check out the video tutorial at https://youtu.be/c4E7QivN5Dw

1. Download and Install VirtualBox
Step 1.1: Download VirtualBox
* 		Go to the official VirtualBox website: https://www.virtualbox.org/
* 		Navigate to the "Downloads" section.
* 		Choose the installer for your operating system (Windows, macOS, Linux).
Step 1.2: Install VirtualBox
* 		Run the downloaded installer.
* 		Follow the installation wizard:
    * 		Accept the license agreement.
    * 		Choose installation options (default settings are usually fine).
    * 		Allow network interfaces installation if prompted.
    * 		Complete the installation and launch VirtualBox.

2. Download Kali Linux ISO
Step 2.1: Get Kali Linux ISO
* 		Visit the official Kali Linux website: https://www.kali.org/get-kali/
* 		Download the appropriate Kali Linux ISO image (usually the 64-bit installer ISO).

3. Create a New Virtual Machine in VirtualBox
Step 3.1: Open VirtualBox and Click "New"
* 		Click the "New" button to create a new VM.
Step 3.2: Name and Operating System
* 		Name: Enter a name like "Kali Linux".
* 		Type: Select "Linux".
* 		Version: Select "Debian (64-bit)" (Kali is based on Debian).
Step 3.3: Allocate Memory (RAM)
* 		Assign RAM to the VM. Kali Linux runs well with at least 2048 MB (2 GB), but 4096 MB (4 GB) is better if your host system allows.
Step 3.4: Create a Virtual Hard Disk
* 		Choose "Create a virtual hard disk now".
* 		Click "Create".
Step 3.5: Hard Disk File Type
* 		Select "VDI (VirtualBox Disk Image)".
* 		Click "Next".
Step 3.6: Storage on Physical Hard Disk
* 		Choose "Dynamically allocated" (grows as needed).
* 		Click "Next".
Step 3.7: Specify Disk Size
* 		Set the disk size (at least 20 GB recommended).
* 		Click "Create".

4. Configure the Kali Linux VM Network to NAT
Step 4.1: Select the Kali VM and Click "Settings"
* 		Go to the "Network" tab.
Step 4.2: Enable Network Adapter
* 		Ensure "Adapter 1" is enabled.
* 		Attached to: Select "NAT".
* 		This setting allows the VM to access the internet through your host machine's network.

5. Mount Kali Linux ISO and Start the VM
Step 5.1: Mount the ISO
* 		In the VM "Settings", go to "Storage".
* 		Under "Controller: IDE" or "Controller: SATA", click the empty optical drive.
* 		Click the disk icon on the right side and choose "Choose a disk file".
* 		Select the Kali Linux ISO you downloaded.
Step 5.2: Start the VM
* 		Click "Start" to boot the VM from the Kali Linux ISO.

6. Install Kali Linux on the Virtual Machine
Step 6.1: Kali Linux Installer
* 		When the Kali boot menu appears, select "Graphical install" or "Install".
* 		Follow the on-screen prompts:
    * 		Select language, location, and keyboard.
    * 		Configure network (you can skip or configure hostname).
    * 		Set up user account and password.
    * 		Partition disks (use guided partitioning with entire disk).
    * 		Confirm and write changes to disk.
    * 		Install the base system and additional software.
    * 		Install GRUB bootloader (choose to install to the master boot record).
    * 		Finish installation and reboot.

7. Final Steps
Step 7.1: Remove Kali ISO from Virtual Drive
* 		After reboot, go back to VM "Settings" → "Storage".
* 		Remove the Kali Linux ISO from the virtual optical drive to avoid booting from it again.
Step 7.2: Start Kali Linux VM
* 		Start the VM normally.
* 		Kali Linux should boot from the virtual hard disk.
Step 7.3: Verify Network Connectivity
* If you are using Kali Linux 2026.1 or higher you need to run the following command 		
* Open a terminal in Kali Linux.
* “ sudo nmcli connection modify “Wired connection 1” ipv4.dad-timeout 0”
* Double click on the connection icon 
* Got to IPV4 settings
* Manually create the address and gateway according to the NAT settings
* Manually set the DNS to 10.0.0.1  or Google’s DNS 8.8.8.8
* 		Test internet connectivity by pinging a website, e.g., ping google.com.
* 		NAT networking should allow internet access through your host.


Step-by-Step Instructions to Create a Snapshot
Step 1: Open VirtualBox and Select Your VM
* 		Launch VirtualBox.
* 		In the list of virtual machines, click to select your Kali Linux VM (or any VM you want to snapshot).
Step 2: Open the Snapshots Pane
* 		With the VM selected, click on the Snapshots button located at the top right of the VirtualBox window. It looks like a camera icon.
* 		Alternatively, right-click the VM and select Snapshots from the context menu.
Step 3: Create a New Snapshot
* 		In the Snapshots pane, click the Take button (usually a camera icon with a plus sign).
* 		A dialog box will appear asking for:
    * 		Name: Enter a descriptive name for the snapshot (e.g., "Clean Install" or "Before Update").
    * 		Description (optional): Add details about the snapshot for future reference.
Step 4: Confirm Snapshot Creation
* 		Click OK to create the snapshot.
* 		VirtualBox will save the current state of the VM. This may take a few moments depending on the VM size and state.

Step 5: Using Snapshots Later
* 		To revert to a snapshot, open the Snapshots pane, select the snapshot you want, and click Restore.
* 		You can also delete snapshots to free up disk space by selecting a snapshot and clicking the Delete button.



